\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Control and quantum dependence in a variational (VQE) ansatz, criterion $q_0$. After the loops, an auxiliary qubit is measured mid-circuit and conditions a flip of $q_0$. (b)~One graph for the rules of Table~\ref {tab:rules}. Each node is an operation as it executes, with its source line and loop iteration, and a box holds each iteration of the outer loop. Grey data edges belong to every rule, and def-use lacks the green control edges. The classical PDG lacks the blue quantum edges and has the dotted edges instead, each of which skips a CNOT whose control it reads as unchanged. The slices are nested, so a node is shaded by the weakest rule that keeps it, and each rule keeps its shade and every darker one. The QDG keeps exactly the dependences of $q_0$, and the QPDG adds the hatched operations, which are not dependences. Graphs, slices and fidelities at $\lvert 0\dots  0\rangle $ are computed by the study's code from the program shown.}}{4}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ansatz}{{2}{4}{}{figure.2}{}}
\citation{svore2018qsharp}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {I}{\ignorespaces The dependence rules, by what a CNOT with control $c$ and target $t$ writes. Every rule reads $\{c, t\}$, and complete means dependence-complete. The last columns give the slice of $q_0$ in Fig.~\ref {fig:ansatz}, as the operations it keeps of 19{} and its fidelity at $\lvert 0\dots  0\rangle $.}}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:rules}{{I}{5}{}{table.1}{}}
